<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tlacochcalcatl</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The tlacochcalcatl was one of the two supreme military commanders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, whose title is associated with the house of darts, or armoury. Together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacatecatl">tlacatecatl</a>, he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> army. Accounts of the Aztec Empire describe these two offices as the military leadership within the empire's system of governance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tlacochcalcatl.jpg" alt="Tlacochcalcatl" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roberto malvido · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teopanzolco_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The title tlacochcalcatl means the man from the house of darts, and the rank is roughly comparable to that of a modern field marshal. In Aztec warfare its holder was second in command only to the tlatoani, the ruler, and usually led the army into battle when the ruler was occupied elsewhere. Once the tlatoani had decided to undertake a campaign, the tlacochcalcatl and the tlacateccatl, or general, took all the military decisions and did the planning.</p><p>The tlacochcalcatl was also in charge of the tlacochcalco, the house of darts: four armouries at the four entrances to the ceremonial precinct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. They were stocked with new weapons every year during the festival of Quecholli, and the Spanish conquistador Andrés de Tapia estimated the weapons in each armoury at 500 cartloads. The holder of the office was always a member of the military order of the cuachicqueh, the shorn ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The office was often the last step towards becoming tlatoani. The first tlacochcalcatl was appointed by Huitzilihuitl, who gave the post to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a>, and Itzcoatl probably also held it under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimalpopoca">Chimalpopoca</a>. On becoming ruler, Itzcoatl named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacaelel">Tlacaelel</a> tlacochcalcatl and Moctezuma Ilhuicamina tlacateccatl; when Tlacaelel was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cihuacoatl">cihuacoatl</a>, Moctezuma Ilhuicamina was promoted in his place.</p><p>It is not known who held the office during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacaelel">Tlacaelel</a> may have held two offices at once in this period. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a>, Moctezuma Ilhuicamina's son and successor, the tlacochcalcatl was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tizoc">Tizoc</a>, who became ruler at Axayacatl's death. Tizoc was seen as a weak ruler and was removed, and his own tlacochcalcatl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>, became ruler in turn. Ahuitzotl's tlacochcalcatl was the next ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>, whose tlacochcalcatl at the arrival of the Spaniards was Quappiatl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tlacochcalcatl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Beneath him stood the cihuacoatl, a chief minister who managed internal affairs; tribute stewards (calpixque) who collected and recorded tribute in the provinces; and the senior commanders, the tlacatecatl and <b>tlacochcalcatl</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
